---
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Terminal from '../../components/Terminal.astro';
import Post from '../../layouts/Post.astro';
---

<Post slug="integrating-atproto">
	<p>
		to me, atproto is that i shouldn't have to pay Elon $10/mo just to do
		data analysis on my twitter posts, or pay GitHub to do CI on my code.
		its that i can also easily use my one account to do things across many
		services. the two stuff im about to talk about here shows what i like
		about atproto.
	</p>

	<h2>pull request previews on tangled</h2>

	<p>
		if you keep your repos on <a href="https://tangled.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">tangled</a>,
		you can have wisp build and deploy a preview site whenever a pull request
		opens or updates, and then you get a comment on that pull request with
		the preview domain.
	</p>

	<figure>
		<img
			src="/images/tangled-pr-preview.png"
			alt="A tangled pull request: the diff of .tangled/workflows/preview.yml on the left, and in the history on the right a comment from wisp.place titled preview deploys, linking the commit to its preview."
		/>
	</figure>

	<Terminal title="preview deploy">
		<span class="t-prompt">$</span> wispctl preview enable nekomimi.pet --repo blog --claim ana
		{'\n\n'}
		<span class="t-success">✓ Enabled previews for blog (claim ana)</span>
		{'\n'}
		<span class="t-muted">The repo still needs WISP_APP_PASSWORD on its spindle and the preview workflow.</span>
	</Terminal>

	<figure>
		<img
			src="/images/dashboard-previews.png"
			alt="The pull-request previews setup in the wisp dashboard, in three steps: tangled ci is connected, previews are on, and the workflow to add to the repo."
		/>
	</figure>

	<p>
		this works on every commit, and to clean old ones up, you can just
		have spindle sweep them on a cron using <code>wispctl site prune</code>.
	</p>

	<h2>automatic DNS with marque.at</h2>

	<p>
		setting up a custom domain on hosts sucks as you have to copy CNAME
		and TXT records into whatever registrar you use. then you have to wait around for
		DNS propagation, while clicking verify repeatedly (well with wisp hopefully not as i
		check your root nameserver directly but i digress).
	</p>

	<p>
		if <a href="marque.at">marque.at</a> runs the domain, wisp can just configure it for you by
	    writing the CNAME and ownership TXT records with just one click. they can do the same too.
		with one click, you can set a wisp site on marque.at's website.
	</p>

	<p>
		i like this for atproto and **you can just do things** (do we still say that?) this way.
	</p>

	<h2>bluesky pbc</h2>
	<p>
		they are the company behind atproto though imo their contribution to the
		rest of us mostly just begins and ends at running the relay for free. beyond
		that how is a .bsky.social supposed to know whats outside the twitter clone?
	</p>
	<p>
		bluesky never features or mentions other atproto
		apps in its client or marketing. they don't use atproto OAuth themselves
		which means 99% of people on the network have never even seen what the
		OAuth looks like or realized their account can log into
		anything else. dame.is's bsky38 showed this brillantly with the amount of
		people who thought it was as scam solely because of the OAuth screen.
	</p>

	<p>
		instead of funding (often unemployed) people who are building libraries and apps,
		<s>they host an annual conference talk</s> (ive since learned its independently run but bsky is
		a major sponsor and helps orchestrate things, still misinformation on my part sorry)
	    which barely leads to us getting jobs,
	    and it doesn't pay server bills or help sustain people. sometimes theyre more likely
		to just shittalk the devs too whether they choose to not use AI, or have disagreeable
		opinions on their poster children
	</p>

	<p>
		and then on top of that, they launch competing *products* that have
		an intention of turning a profit while ignoring community projects they could easily integrate with.
		this week they launched "Sites" in Attie, their chatbot. (anyone whos proai in this space already
		has a favorite chatbot they'd rather use) seemingly to compete with openai on chatgpt sites, but
		they just forgot to build it on AT. the sites are not on your account.
		they don't talk to existing static site projects (tangled and mine, lichen, theres more),
		nate wrote a really good post about this pattern in
		 <a href="https://half-baked.pckt.blog/thoughts-on-attie-qcp39a9" target="_blank" rel="noopener"> thoughts on attie</a>,
		and a lot of devs building on atproto feel the same.
	</p>

	<p>
		wisp talks to tangled and marque because
		we actually care about composing things. bsky
		doesn't do that, so that leaves us to just do it ourselves and not expect much from the big company.
	</p>
</Post>
